Yes! **ROS2 does support processing multiple images from the same topic in parallel** using its executor and callback group mechanisms. Here's how to implement it:

## Solution Overview

You can process multiple incoming images concurrently by using:
1. **`MultiThreadedExecutor`** - for multithreaded execution of callbacks
2. **`ReentrantCallbackGroup`** - allows parallel execution of callbacks from the same topic

## Implementation Steps

### 1. Create a MultiThreaded Executor in your main function

```cpp
#include <rclcpp/rclcpp.hpp>
#include <sensor_msgs/msg/image.hpp>

using namespace rclcpp;

int main(int argc, char * argv[]) {
    rclcpp::init(argc, argv);

    // Create a multithreaded executor
    MultiThreadedExecutor executor;

    // Add your node to this executor
    NodeOptions options;
    options.threads = 4; // Number of threads for processing
    auto node = std::make_shared<Node>(options);

    executor.add_node(node);

    rclcpp::spin(executor);
    rclcpp::shutdown();
    return 0;
}
```

### 2. Use Reentrant Callback Group in your Node

```cpp
class ImageProcessorNode : public Node {
public:
    ImageProcessorNode() : Node("image_processor") {
        // Create a reentrant callback group (allows parallel execution)
        auto callback_group = create_callback_group(
            CallbackGroupType::Reentrant);

        // Create subscription with the reentrant callback group
        rclcpp::SubscriptionOptions options;
        options.callback_group = callback_group;

        my_subscription = create_subscription<sensor_msgs::msg::Image>(
            "/camera/image", 
            SensorDataQoS(), 
            &ImageProcessorNode::image_callback, 
            options);
    }

private:
    void image_callback(const sensor_msgs::msg::Image::SharedPtr msg) {
        // Process the image here
        // Since it's a reentrant callback group, this can run in parallel
        // with other images being processed
        std::cout << "Processing image at time: " 
                  << msg->header.stamp.toSec() << std::endl;

        // Your image processing code here
    }
};
```

## Important Considerations

### ⚠️ Thread Safety
The **ReentrantCallbackGroup** allows parallel execution, but you must handle synchronization yourself if your callbacks access shared variables:

```cpp
class ImageProcessorNode : public Node {
public:
    // Shared data that needs protection
    std::mutex image_mutex;

private:
    void image_callback(const sensor_msgs::msg::Image::SharedPtr msg) {
        // Lock before accessing shared resources
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(image_mutex);

        // Process the image safely
        std::cout << "Processing image at time: " 
                  << msg->header.stamp.toSec() << std::endl;
    }
};
```

### Why Your Initial Attempt Didn't Work

Based on the search results, there were two common issues:
1. **Using `MutuallyExclusiveCallbackGroup` instead of `ReentrantCallbackGroup`** - This prevents parallel execution
2. **Not using a MultiThreadedExecutor** - The default executor is single-threaded

## Alternative: Custom Thread Pool

If you need more control over thread management, you can create your own thread pool:

```cpp
// Create a custom thread pool for image processing
std::vector<std::thread> threads;
std::queue<std::function<void()>> task_queue;

void process_image(const sensor_msgs::msg::Image::SharedPtr msg) {
    // Your image processing logic
}

// Start threads to process images from the queue
for (int i = 0; i < num_threads; ++i) {
    threads.emplace_back([this]() {
        while (!task_queue.empty()) {
            auto task = std::move(task_queue.front());
            task_queue.pop();
            task();
        }
    });
}
```

## Summary

| Component | Purpose |
|-----------|---------|
| `MultiThreadedExecutor` | Enables parallel callback execution across threads |
| `ReentrantCallbackGroup` | Allows callbacks from the same topic to run concurrently |
| Manual synchronization (`std::mutex`) | Protects shared data accessed by multiple threads |

This approach is **recommended for your use case** where images arrive faster than they can be processed. The ROS2 executor will automatically distribute work across threads, and you only need to ensure thread safety for shared resources.

